        "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14099242/assets/b444409245ad52ba5bafe15f8ca354f2daa7caea3e0c5fb46e4d71a62dcf66d7.png)\n\nThe shaded regions above represent the truth sets of the predicates $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y),$ defined over the universal set ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following is the truth set of $P\u2227Q?$",
        "worked_solution": "The truth set of $P\u2227Q$ represents the *intersection* of the truth sets of $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y):$\n\n$$\n{T}_{P\u2227Q}={T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to get the truth set of $P(x,y)\u2228Q(x,y),$ we take all the points that belong to both ${T}_{P}$ and ${T}_{Q},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14099242/assets/09ccd14aa66859f0e61997975c6c5ea4d760d4b575236533079289036728b821.png)\n\n**Note:** The points where the *dashed* line segment meets the *solid* curve do not belong to ${T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q}$, since these points are *not* in  ${T}_{Q}.$",
